    <h2 id="examples">Invoice examples for freelancers and small businesses</h2>
    <p>A good <strong>freelance invoice</strong> describes the work in words your client's finance team understands. Here is how the line items might look for common jobs.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Web developer:</strong> "Marketing site, 5 pages, responsive build" (fixed price), or "Development, 24 hours at $60" (hourly).</li>
      <li><strong>Graphic designer:</strong> "Brand identity: logo, colour palette, typography" as one line, with "Revisions, additional round" as a separate line if the client asks for more.</li>
      <li><strong>Consultant:</strong> "Strategy workshop, 1 day" and "Follow-up report", with expenses added as their own lines or under shipping and fees.</li>
      <li><strong>Copywriter or translator:</strong> "Blog articles, 4 &times; $90" or "Translation, 3,200 words at $0.08".</li>
      <li><strong>Agency or studio:</strong> a deposit invoice for 50 percent, then a final invoice that shows the deposit under "Amount already paid".</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Fixed price or hourly, the rule is the same: one clear line per deliverable, a quantity, and a rate. Vague lines like "services" are the ones that get queried.</p>

    <h2 id="languages">Invoices in Arabic, Persian and other languages</h2>
    <p>Working with international clients often means sending documents in their language. <a href="/products/invoice-studio-pro.html">Invoice Studio</a>'s document labels come in <strong>seven languages</strong>: English, Spanish, French, German, Portuguese, Arabic and Persian. Arabic and Persian documents switch to a proper <strong>right-to-left layout</strong>, and Persian dates use the Persian locale.</p>
    <p>Only the built-in labels change, such as "Invoice", "Due" and "Total". The descriptions, notes and terms you type stay exactly as you wrote them, so you can mix languages when a client needs it.</p>

    <figure>
      <img src="/blog/images/persian-rtl-invoice.webp" width="900" height="667" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="Persian invoice with right-to-left layout, Persian labels and dates, and a paid stamp, created offline with Invoice Studio">
      <figcaption>A Persian invoice with right-to-left layout, Persian labels and dates, and a paid stamp.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h2 id="how-to">How to create an invoice in Invoice Studio</h2>
    <p>The whole process takes a few minutes once your business details are saved.</p>
    <figure>
      <img src="/blog/images/invoice-studio-editor.webp" width="1400" height="875" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="Invoice Studio editor: invoice form on the left and a live A4 invoice preview with VAT, discount and amount due on the right">
      <figcaption>The editor on the left, a live A4 preview on the right. Everything you type updates the invoice instantly.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <ol>
      <li><strong>Open the file.</strong> Double-click <code>invoice-studio.html</code>. It opens in your browser, with no installation.</li>
      <li><strong>Enter your business details once.</strong> Name, address, email, tax number and an optional logo. They are remembered next time.</li>
      <li><strong>Add the client.</strong> Type their details, and press "Save this client" so you can pick them again later with one click.</li>
      <li><strong>Add line items.</strong> Description, quantity and rate. Drag lines to reorder them. Negative rates work for credits.</li>
      <li><strong>Set tax, discount and shipping</strong> if they apply.</li>
      <li><strong>Add payment details.</strong> Bank instructions, crypto wallets, or both.</li>
      <li><strong>Choose a look.</strong> Four templates (Classic, Modern, Compact and Typed) and eight accent colors.</li>
      <li><strong>Save and export.</strong> Press Save to keep the document, then Print / PDF and choose "Save as PDF". For the cleanest result, turn on background graphics in the print dialog.</li>
    </ol>
    <figure>
      <img src="/blog/images/invoice-templates.webp" width="1400" height="1029" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="The four invoice templates in Invoice Studio: Classic, Modern, Compact and Typed, each with a different accent color">
      <figcaption>Classic, Modern, Compact and Typed. Each template works with any of the eight accent colors.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>Saved documents can be reopened, duplicated as a new draft (handy for recurring clients), or exported as a CSV list for your bookkeeping.</p>

    <h2 id="limits">What an offline invoice maker doesn't do</h2>
    <p>No tool is right for everyone, so here is where Invoice Studio stops:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>It doesn't send email.</strong> You attach the PDF yourself.</li>
      <li><strong>It doesn't sync between devices.</strong> Your data lives in one browser. Use the built-in backup export and import to move it.</li>
      <li><strong>It doesn't track payments automatically.</strong> You change a document's status to Sent, Paid or Overdue yourself.</li>
      <li><strong>It isn't full accounting software.</strong> It creates documents and a simple list. It doesn't do ledgers, payroll or tax filing.</li>
    </ul>
    <h3>Back up your data</h3>
    <p>Because everything is stored in your browser, clearing browser data or switching computers can erase it. Use the Backup section to export a file every so often. Restoring is one click. Ten seconds of habit protects your entire billing history.</p>
    <p>If those limits are fine for you, Invoice Studio suits freelancers, consultants, designers, developers, small agencies and anyone who bills in more than one currency or accepts crypto and wants a private, subscription-free tool.</p>

    <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>

